window.VYNE_SEARCH = [{"id":"intro","title":"Introduction","group":"Getting Started","path":"index.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"what-is-vyne","text":"What Vyne is"},{"level":2,"id":"design-goals","text":"Design goals"},{"level":2,"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know first"},{"level":2,"id":"how-the-compiler-runs","text":"How the compiler runs"},{"level":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next"}],"text":"Active development · v0.0.4 The Vyne language A small, statically typed language with arena-based memory , explicit lexical regions , and a C transpiler backend. No GC pauses. No manual free . Build games, audio tools, and web servers from one toolchain. 0 GC pauses 1 compiled target: C 11 standard modules ~3ms hello-world compile What Vyne is Vyne is a general-purpose programming language designed around one idea: memory lifetime should be visible in the source code, and it should not be a garbage collector's job . You tell the compiler where a chunk of work begins and where its scratch memory ends. The runtime does the rest. The language draws its surface syntax from C, Lua, and Python — C-style operators, Lua-style iteration with through ... loop , and Python-like string formatting. Under the hood, every Vyne program is lowered to a single C translation unit and handed to a C compiler. That means Vyne runs at native speed without a runtime interpreter , and it interops trivially with any C library you can link. Design goals Predictable memory Regions give you explicit control over when scratch memory is reclaimed. No GC sweeps, no pauses, no surprises. Small surface One file format, one type system, no metaprogramming, no macros. Learn the whole language in an afternoon. Native code Everything compiles to C, which compiles to machine code. No interpreter overhead, no JIT warm-up. What you need to know first This manual assumes you are comfortable with at least one curly-brace language (C, JavaScript, Go, Rust, or similar). If you can read for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) { ... } and know what null means, you are ready. No interpreter mode As of v0.0.4, Vyne only compiles to C. Earlier releases also shipped a tree-walking interpreter for fast iteration; that path has been removed. If you find a reference to --interp anywhere in the docs, it is stale — please report it. How the compiler runs You write .vy files. The vynec compiler runs three stages: Lexer + parser. Source text becomes an abstract syntax tree. Semantic analysis. Types are checked, symbols resolved, regions validated. Diagnostics are reported with source-context carets. C emission. The AST is lowered into a single C translation unit that includes vyne_runtime.h and any module headers you imported. That C file is then compiled by your system C compiler (GCC, Clang, or MSVC) and linked into a standalone binary. You never see the generated C unless you want to. The compiler cleans it up by default. Where to go next If you want to start writing code immediately, jump to Hello, World . If you want to understand the whole language before writing anything, Language Tour is a single pass over every construct you will meet in practice."},{"id":"install","title":"Installation","group":"Getting Started","path":"pages/install.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites"},{"level":2,"id":"build-from-source","text":"Build from source"},{"level":2,"id":"compiling-a-program","text":"Compiling a program"}],"text":"Getting Started Installation Vyne has no runtime dependencies beyond a C compiler. Install once, use everywhere. Prerequisites A C11-compatible compiler: GCC 11+ , Clang 14+ , or MSVC 19.30+ . make (or an equivalent build tool for your platform). Linux, macOS, or Windows 10+. Build from source shell copy # Clone git clone https://github.com/t2ncay/vyne.git # Build the compiler cd vyne make # Add to PATH (Linux / macOS) export PATH=\"$PWD/bin:$PATH\" After this, vynec is on your path. Verify it: shell copy vynec --version # → vynec v0.0.4 (C backend) Compiling a program The compiler takes a .vy file and produces an executable: shell copy vynec hello.vy ./hello Two flags are worth remembering on day one: Flag Effect --emit-c Write the intermediate C file to disk for inspection. --keep Keep intermediate artifacts (the .c file and object files) after linking. On first compile The first time you compile any program, vynec will build its runtime support headers into a local cache. This adds a few seconds to the first build and nothing to subsequent ones."},{"id":"hello","title":"Hello, World","group":"Getting Started","path":"pages/hello.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"reading-it","text":"Reading it line by line"},{"level":2,"id":"with-a-variable","text":"With a variable"}],"text":"Getting Started Hello, World The smallest Vyne program is one line. hello.vy vyne copy out ( \"hello, world\" ); Save it as hello.vy , then: shell copy vynec hello.vy ./hello # → hello, world Reading it line by line out is a built-in function. It takes any value, converts it to its display form, and writes it to standard output followed by a newline. It is not a keyword — it is defined by the runtime — but it is globally available and never needs to be declared. \"hello, world\" is a string literal. Strings in Vyne are immutable and UTF-8. The trailing ; is a statement terminator. Vyne does not have automatic semicolon insertion, so you always write it. With a variable hello2.vy vyne copy // `::` declares the type; the value infers the rest. greeting :: String = \"hello\" ; name :: String = \"world\" ; out (greeting + \", \" + name + \"!\" ); :: is Vyne's type-annotation operator. It reads naturally: \"greeting is a String\". Once a variable has a type, its storage layout is fixed for its lifetime — reassigning a String to an Int64 will be a compile error. String concatenation The + operator concatenates when at least one side is a String . This is the only implicit coercion Vyne performs; everything else requires an explicit conversion with string() , int64() , or float64() ."},{"id":"tour","title":"Language Tour","group":"Getting Started","path":"pages/tour.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"tour-values","text":"Values"},{"level":2,"id":"tour-control","text":"Control flow"},{"level":2,"id":"tour-functions","text":"Functions"},{"level":2,"id":"tour-structs","text":"Structs via interface"},{"level":2,"id":"tour-regions","text":"Memory and regions"},{"level":2,"id":"tour-errors","text":"Error handling"},{"level":2,"id":"tour-modules","text":"Modules"}],"text":"Getting Started Language Tour One page, every construct. Read this end-to-end and you will recognise any Vyne file you meet. Values Vyne has four primitive types — Int64 , Float64 , Bool , Null — and four composite ones: String , Array , Map , and Struct (declared with the interface keyword). tour.vy — values vyne copy count :: Int64 = 42 ; ratio :: Float64 = 3.14 ; ready :: Bool = true ; nothing :: Null = null ; name :: String = \"vyne\" ; items :: Array = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; pair :: Map = { \"x\" : 1 , \"y\" : 2 }; Control flow Familiar if and while , plus a distinctive iteration construct called through . There are five modes: loop (side effects), collect (map), filter , every (all), and unique (dedupe). tour.vy — control vyne copy if (count > 10 ) { out ( \"big\" ); } else { out ( \"small\" ); }; // Loop over a range, side effects only through i :: 1 .. 5 -> loop { out (i); }; // Map: multiply every element by 2 doubled :: Array = through x :: [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] -> collect { x * 2 }; // Filter: keep only even numbers evens :: Array = through n :: 0 .. 10 -> filter { n % 2 == 0 }; Functions Declared with fn . Parameters can be typed with :: ; return type after -> . tour.vy — functions vyne copy fn add (a :: Int64 , b :: Int64 ) -> Int64 { return a + b; }; // Return type and param types are optional in dynamic mode fn double (x) { x * 2 }; out ( add ( 2 , 3 )); // → 5 out ( double ( 7 )); // → 14 Structs via interface Vyne has no separate struct or class keyword. An interface block declares fields and methods together, and doubles as a positional constructor. tour.vy — interface vyne copy interface Circle { r :: Float64 ; area() -> Float64 { return r * r * 3.14159 ; }; }; c :: Circle = Circle ( 4.0 ); out (c.area()); // → 50.26544 Memory and regions Regions are the reason Vyne exists. A region block marks a lexical scope whose scratch allocations are released on exit. Values you need to keep past the region boundary are marked with region.commit . tour.vy — regions vyne copy through epoch :: 1 .. 1000 -> loop { region scratch { xs :: Array = [epoch, epoch + 1 , epoch + 2 ]; msg :: String = \"epoch:\" + string (epoch); // msg survives the region; xs does not. region.commit (msg); }; out (msg); }; On every iteration, everything allocated inside scratch is reclaimed at the closing brace — except msg , which was committed. Total memory usage stays flat regardless of iteration count. Error handling try / catch / finally work the way you expect, with a jump-buffer implementation. tour.vy — errors vyne copy try { risky(); throw \"boom\" ; } catch (err) { out ( \"caught: \" + err); } finally { out ( \"cleanup\" ); }; Modules The module keyword pulls a built-in library into scope. group organises your own code into namespaces. use imports another file. tour.vy — modules vyne copy module vmath; module vcore; r :: Float64 = vmath.sqrt( 16.0 ); out (r); // → 4.0 That is the whole language in a page. The remaining chapters in Language Reference expand each section with edge cases and formal rules."},{"id":"types","title":"Values & Types","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/types.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"primitive-types","text":"Primitive types"},{"level":2,"id":"composite-types","text":"Composite types"},{"level":2,"id":"typed-vs-untyped-arrays","text":"Typed vs. untyped arrays"},{"level":2,"id":"strings","text":"Strings"},{"level":2,"id":"null-and-absence","text":"Null and absence"},{"level":2,"id":"type-inference","text":"Type inference"}],"text":"Language Reference Values & Types Vyne has eight value kinds. Four are primitives stored inline; four are composites stored behind a reference. Primitive types Type Storage Range / notes Int64 8 bytes, signed −2⁶³ to 2⁶³ − 1 Float64 8 bytes, IEEE 754 Double precision Bool 1 byte true / false Null 0 bytes of payload The absence of a value Primitives are copied by value at every assignment, return, and parameter pass. There is no boxing overhead for primitives used in their native type — the compiler emits int64_t , double , or bool directly. Composite types Type Notes String UTF-8, immutable. Stored as a pointer to a NUL-terminated byte sequence. Array Heterogeneous, growable. Elements are 16-byte tagged values. Array<T> Typed, unboxed. Array<Int64> and Array<Float64> store elements as raw int64_t / double . Significantly faster and smaller than the generic Array . Map String keys only. Open-addressed hash table with tombstones. Iteration order is unspecified . Struct Declared via interface . Contains named fields and method bindings. Typed vs. untyped arrays This is the single most consequential typing decision you will make. Array (boxed) Each element is a 16-byte tagged union. Any value of any type can live alongside any other. Convenient, slower, larger. Array<T> (typed) Elements stored contiguously in native layout. Array<Float64> of length N occupies exactly 8N bytes. Ideal for numerics. arrays.vy vyne copy // Boxed — mixed types allowed mixed :: Array = [ 1 , \"two\" , 3.0 ]; // Typed — all elements same type, unboxed xs :: Array<Int64> = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ]; ys :: Array<Float64> = [ 1.0 , 2.0 , 3.0 ]; // Both share the same indexing syntax out (xs[ 0 ]); // → 1 How to decide If every element has the same numeric type, always prefer Array<T> . Fall back to plain Array only when you genuinely need heterogeneous elements (or a dynamic type the compiler cannot prove). Strings Strings are immutable. Every operation that \"modifies\" a string returns a new one. The compiler picks one of two storage strategies: Static strings — literals like \"hello\" are emitted into read-only memory. Free, no allocation, no lifetime concern. Arena strings — the result of concatenation, substring, formatting, and so on, lives in the current arena. Its lifetime is bounded by the enclosing region. From Vyne source, this distinction is not observable . \"hello\" and \"hel\" + \"lo\" produce values that compare equal and behave identically. But the latter allocates, and if you are inside a hot loop, that matters. Null and absence null is a first-class value. Reading an out-of-bounds array index yields null , as does reading a missing map key. The null-coalescing operator ?? and its assignment form ??= exist to make handling this ergonomic: null.vy vyne copy name :: String = lookup( \"missing\" ) ?? \"anonymous\" ; cfg.mode ??= \"default\" ; // assign only if currently null Type inference The compiler infers a variable's type from its initializer when you write = alone. The :: operator asserts a type explicitly. Under strict mode (the default), you must use :: ; dynamic mode relaxes this. inference.vy vyne copy x = 42 ; // x : Int64, inferred y :: Float64 = 42 ; // y : Float64, explicit (implicit int → float widening)"},{"id":"expr","title":"Literals & Expressions","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/expr.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"numeric-literals","text":"Numeric literals"},{"level":2,"id":"string-literals","text":"String literals"},{"level":2,"id":"interpolated-strings","text":"Interpolated strings"},{"level":2,"id":"array-literals","text":"Array literals"},{"level":2,"id":"map-literals","text":"Map literals"},{"level":2,"id":"range-literals","text":"Ranges"},{"level":2,"id":"operators","text":"Operators"},{"level":3,"id":"arithmetic","text":"Arithmetic"},{"level":3,"id":"comparison","text":"Comparison"},{"level":3,"id":"logical","text":"Logical"},{"level":3,"id":"membership","text":"Membership"},{"level":3,"id":"pipeline","text":"Pipeline"},{"level":3,"id":"ternary-nullcoalesce","text":"Ternary and null-coalesce"}],"text":"Language Reference Literals & Expressions Numeric, string, array, map, and range literals, plus the full operator set. Numeric literals numbers.vy vyne copy 42 // Int64 3.14 // Float64 0.5 // Float64 Vyne does not currently support hexadecimal, binary, or scientific-notation literals in source. Use vcore.hex_to_int64(\"0xFF\") when you need to read a hex value from text. String literals Strings use double quotes. Escape sequences are C-compatible: Escape Meaning \\n Newline \\t Tab \\r Carriage return \\\\ Backslash \\\" Double quote \\{ \\} Literal braces (suppresses interpolation) \\ooo Up to 3 octal digits Interpolated strings Any {expr} inside a string is evaluated and converted to text: interp.vy vyne copy name :: String = \"Ada\" ; score :: Int64 = 99 ; out ( \"Player {name} scored {score}\" ); // → Player Ada scored 99 Array literals arrays.vy vyne copy empty :: Array = []; numbers :: Array = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; mixed :: Array = [ 1 , \"two\" , 3.0 , null ]; If every element is the same numeric type ( Int64 or Float64 ), the compiler materialises the literal as a typed, unboxed array. Otherwise, it falls back to the boxed form. Map literals maps.vy vyne copy point :: Map = { \"x\" : 1 , \"y\" : 2 }; out (point[ \"x\" ]); // → 1 Keys must be string literals at the literal site. Runtime lookup with a computed string key is fine — m[keyVar] — but the map literal syntax itself only accepts literal strings. Ranges The .. operator constructs an inclusive range. It is most useful inside through : ranges.vy vyne copy through i :: 1 .. 10 -> loop { out (i); }; // As a value, it produces an array xs :: Array = 1 .. 5 ; // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] Operators Vyne inherits C's operator set and adds a handful of its own. Precedence, lowest to highest: Level Operators Associativity 1 ?: right 2 ?? right 3 |> left 4 || left 5 && left 6 == != left 7 in left 8 < <= > >= left 9 .. left 10 + - // % left 11 * / ** left 12 ! - $ prefix 13 ++ -- postfix Arithmetic + - * / are the familiar four. Two extras: // — floor division . Truncates toward negative infinity on Int64 (matching C's / is not what it does — Vyne is C-truncating here for consistency; the name is historical). % — modulo. Always matches the sign of the left operand. ** — exponentiation. Always returns Float64 , even for integer operands. Comparison == performs deep equality on composite values: two arrays with the same elements are equal, two maps with the same key-value pairs are equal. For reference types you do not get pointer identity unless both sides are the same underlying value. Logical && and || short-circuit. Any value is truthy except null , false , 0 , 0.0 , and the empty string. Membership x in collection works for arrays (element search), maps (key search), and strings (substring test). Pipeline value |> fn(args) is sugar for fn(value, args) . It lets you write data-flow chains without nesting: pipeline.vy vyne copy // These are equivalent: result :: Array = sort(filter(xs, isPositive)); result :: Array = xs |> filter(isPositive) |> sort(); Ternary and null-coalesce ternary.vy vyne copy label :: String = ready ? \"go\" : \"wait\" ; display :: String = name ?? \"anonymous\" ;"},{"id":"vars","title":"Variables & Assignment","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/vars.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"declaration-syntax","text":"Declaration syntax"},{"level":2,"id":"reassignment","text":"Reassignment"},{"level":2,"id":"constants","text":"Constants"},{"level":2,"id":"indexed-assignment","text":"Indexed assignment"},{"level":2,"id":"no-destructuring","text":"No destructuring"},{"level":2,"id":"scope-rules","text":"Scope"}],"text":"Language Reference Variables & Assignment Declaration, type annotation, reassignment, and mutability rules. Declaration syntax The form is name :: Type = expression; . The type annotation is mandatory under strict mode and optional under dynamic mode. The initializer is optional only when the type has a default (primitives, empty array, empty map, empty string). vars.vy vyne copy // Explicit count :: Int64 = 0 ; label :: String = \"\" ; xs :: Array = []; // Default-initialised (no initializer) ready :: Bool ; // false total :: Int64 ; // 0 ratio :: Float64 ; // 0.0 // Inferred (dynamic mode only) x = 42 ; // x : Int64 Reassignment Use = to reassign an existing variable. Its type is fixed at declaration; assigning a value of a different type is a compile error. reassign.vy vyne copy x :: Int64 = 1 ; x = 2 ; // OK x = \"hello\" ; // Compile error: cannot assign String to Int64 Constants Prefix a declaration with const to make it read-only. Reassignment of a const is a compile error. const.vy vyne copy const pi :: Float64 = 3.14159 ; // pi = 3.0; // Compile error Indexed assignment Arrays and maps support element assignment with the same [] syntax used for reads. indexed.vy vyne copy xs :: Array = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; xs[ 0 ] = 99 ; // OK m :: Map = { \"a\" : 1 }; m[ \"b\" ] = 2 ; // inserts \"b\" Out-of-bounds writes are silent Writing to an array index that is out of range is a no-op — it does not throw, grow the array, or report an error. Always ensure your index is valid before writing. No destructuring Vyne does not support destructuring assignment. The following is not valid: nope.vy vyne copy // NOT valid Vyne [x, y] = pair; // ✗ { a, b } = map; // ✗ Assign each element individually instead. Scope Variables live in the block where they are declared. Blocks are introduced by { ... } , function bodies, loop bodies, and region bodies. A variable declared inside a block is not visible outside it. scope.vy vyne copy x :: Int64 = 1 ; { y :: Int64 = 2 ; out (x + y); // → 3, outer visible }; // out(y); // Compile error: y not in scope"},{"id":"flow","title":"Control Flow","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/flow.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"if-statements","text":"if / else"},{"level":2,"id":"while-loops","text":"while"},{"level":2,"id":"through-loops","text":"through — the iteration construct"},{"level":3,"id":"through-loop","text":"loop"},{"level":3,"id":"through-collect","text":"collect"},{"level":3,"id":"through-filter","text":"filter"},{"level":3,"id":"through-every","text":"every"},{"level":3,"id":"through-unique","text":"unique"},{"level":2,"id":"break-continue","text":"break and continue"},{"level":2,"id":"ternary","text":"Ternary expression"}],"text":"Language Reference Control Flow Conditionals, loops, and Vyne's distinctive through iteration. if / else Conditions must be parenthesised. Braces are required for both branches; there is no single-statement form. if.vy vyne copy if (score >= 90 ) { out ( \"A\" ); } else if (score >= 80 ) { out ( \"B\" ); } else { out ( \"C\" ); }; while while.vy vyne copy i :: Int64 = 0 ; while (i < 10 ) { out (i); i ++ ; }; through — the iteration construct Vyne's through is a general iteration primitive that bundles looping with collection production. The syntactic shape is: through name :: iterable -> mode { body }; The five modes have distinct semantics: Mode Body result used for Returns loop — last body value (rarely used) collect new array element Array of body results filter keep/discard predicate Array of kept source elements every predicate, short-circuits on first false Bool unique — Array of deduplicated source elements loop Side effects only. Most common when you want to do something N times. loop.vy vyne copy through i :: 1 .. 5 -> loop { out ( \"iteration \" + string (i)); }; collect Transform each element. The body's value becomes the corresponding output element. collect.vy vyne copy squares :: Array = through x :: 1 .. 5 -> collect { x * x }; // → [1, 4, 9, 16, 25] filter Keep source elements for which the body is truthy. filter.vy vyne copy evens :: Array = through n :: 0 .. 10 -> filter { n % 2 == 0 }; // → [0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10] every Does every element satisfy the predicate? Short-circuits at the first false. every.vy vyne copy allPositive :: Bool = through x :: [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] -> every { x > 0 }; // → true unique Deduplicate preserving first-seen order. unique.vy vyne copy distinct :: Array = through x :: [ 1 , 2 , 1 , 3 , 2 ] -> unique { }; // → [1, 2, 3] break and continue Both work inside any loop. Both must unwind any live regions on the way out — that happens automatically: break.vy vyne copy through i :: 1 .. 100 -> loop { region scratch { xs :: Array = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; if (i == 5 ) { break ; }; }; }; break / continue inside try — not supported You cannot use break or continue to exit a try block. The compiler rejects this with a clear error. Use a flag variable and check it after the try/catch/finally has run. Ternary expression cond ? a : b is an expression, not a statement — usable anywhere a value is expected."},{"id":"fn","title":"Functions","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/fn.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"declaration","text":"Declaration"},{"level":2,"id":"return","text":"return"},{"level":2,"id":"named-arguments","text":"Named arguments"},{"level":2,"id":"recursion","text":"Recursion"},{"level":2,"id":"type-parameters","text":"Type parameters experimental"},{"level":2,"id":"interfaces-as-structs","text":"Functions on interfaces"}],"text":"Language Reference Functions Declaration, parameters, return values, and named arguments. Declaration Functions start with fn . The parameter list can annotate each parameter's type with :: . The return type follows -> . Both are optional; missing types default to dynamic. fn.vy vyne copy fn add (a :: Int64 , b :: Int64 ) -> Int64 { return a + b; }; // No types — dynamic mode only fn square (x) { x * x }; // Explicit null return fn log (msg :: String ) -> Null { out (msg); }; return A function without an explicit return returns null . A bare return; is equivalent to return null; . If a function is inside a region , returning a non-primitive value is handled specially — see Memory & Regions . Named arguments At the call site, you can supply arguments by name. You cannot mix positional and named arguments in the same call. named.vy vyne copy fn draw_box (x :: Int64 , y :: Int64 , w :: Int64 , h :: Int64 ) -> Null { // ... }; // Both valid: draw_box ( 10 , 20 , 100 , 50 ); draw_box (x: 10 , y: 20 , w: 100 , h: 50 ); Recursion Functions can call themselves. Vyne does not perform tail-call optimisation, so deep recursion is bounded by the C stack. Type parameters experimental Function-level generic parameters exist syntactically ( fn max<T>(...) ) but the emitter's instantiation strategy is not yet complete. Do not depend on it in production code; the syntax may change before v0.1. Functions on interfaces Methods are declared inside interface blocks — see the next chapter."},{"id":"iface","title":"Interfaces","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/iface.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"declaration","text":"Declaration"},{"level":2,"id":"this-pointer","text":"self"},{"level":2,"id":"field-access","text":"Field access and mutation"},{"level":2,"id":"module-binding","text":"Module binding"},{"level":2,"id":"introspection","text":"Introspection"},{"level":2,"id":"typed-array-fields","text":"Typed array fields"},{"level":2,"id":"value-semantics","text":"Value semantics"}],"text":"Language Reference Interfaces (Structs) Vyne's only user-defined composite type. Doubles as a positional constructor. Declaration An interface block declares named fields and, optionally, methods. The block itself becomes a constructor callable with positional arguments. shapes.vy vyne copy interface Circle { r :: Float64 ; area() -> Float64 { return r * r * 3.14159 ; }; circumference() -> Float64 { return 2.0 * 3.14159 * r; }; }; c :: Circle = Circle ( 4.0 ); out (c.area()); // → 50.26544 out (c.circumference()); // → 25.13272 self Inside a method, self refers to the receiver. Field access is implicit — you can read r directly — but the compiler will also accept self.r for clarity. Field access and mutation mutate.vy vyne copy c :: Circle = Circle ( 1.0 ); out (c.r); // → 1.0 c.r = 5.0 ; // field assignment out (c.area()); // → 78.53975 Module binding An interface declared inside a group or with an explicit :: Module suffix becomes part of that namespace. grouped.vy vyne copy group Shapes { interface Point { x :: Float64 ; y :: Float64 ; }; }; p :: Shapes.Point = Shapes.Point ( 1.0 , 2.0 ); Introspection obj.fields() returns an array of field names as strings. Useful for debugging and generic serialisation. Typed array fields Fields can be declared as Array<T> for unboxed storage: typed-fields.vy vyne copy interface Matrix { data :: Array<Float64> ; rows :: Int64 ; cols :: Int64 ; }; Value semantics Structs behave as values: assigning one struct to another copies its fields. Methods are shared — the method table is not duplicated. Deep copy on assignment Struct assignment deep-copies any nested arrays or maps. This can be expensive for large structs. If you need shared mutation, wrap the struct in a container or use Array / Map fields, which follow normal array/map reference semantics."},{"id":"mods","title":"Groups & Modules","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/mods.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"module-declaration","text":"module — pull in a built-in"},{"level":2,"id":"group-declaration","text":"group — user namespaces"},{"level":2,"id":"use-import","text":"use — import another file"},{"level":2,"id":"use-lib","text":"use lib — external modules"},{"level":2,"id":"deploy-dismiss","text":"deploy and dismiss"}],"text":"Language Reference Groups & Modules Namespacing, imports, and the built-in module system. module — pull in a built-in Every built-in library must be declared before use. A module statement brings its names into scope. modules.vy vyne copy module vcore; module vmath; out (vmath.sqrt( 16.0 )); group — user namespaces Groups organise related declarations. They can nest. groups.vy vyne copy group Physics { gravity :: Float64 = 9.81 ; group Constants { lightSpeed :: Float64 = 299792458.0 ; }; }; out (Physics.gravity); out (Physics.Constants.lightSpeed); use — import another file use reads a .vy file and executes it in a fresh module scope. Its top-level bindings become members of a namespace named after the file stem. main.vy vyne copy use \"./math_utils.vy\" ; out (math_utils.double( 7 )); Or bind under an alias: main.vy vyne copy use \"./math_utils.vy\" as m; out (m.double( 7 )); use lib — external modules use lib \"...\" loads an external module from the install directory. Paths are resolved relative to the vyne/modules/external/ folder in your Vyne installation. deploy and dismiss For lifecycle control of loaded modules: Statement Effect deploy name; Marks a module as used; makes its members visible. dismiss name; Removes a module from the current scope. In practice The compiler resolves all imports at build time. deploy and dismiss are mostly relevant when writing tools that load Vyne modules dynamically; for typical programs, module and use are all you need."},{"id":"enums","title":"Enums","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/enums.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"enum-syntax","text":"Syntax"},{"level":2,"id":"enum-semantics","text":"Semantics"},{"level":2,"id":"using-enums","text":"Using an enum"}],"text":"Language Reference Enums Named Int64 constants. Auto-numbering with explicit overrides. Syntax enums.vy vyne copy enum Direction { North, // 0 East, // 1 South = 10 , // 10 (resets counter) West // 11 }; out (Direction.South); // → 10 Semantics The backing type is always Int64 . Members are const — reassignment is a compile error. An explicit = N resets the auto-increment counter from that point. Enums are global. There is no namespacing beyond the enum name itself. Using an enum use-enum.vy vyne copy heading :: Int64 = Direction.North; if (heading == Direction.North) { out ( \"heading north\" ); }; Because enum members are plain Int64, you can store them in typed arrays, compare them with integers, and pass them anywhere an Int64 is expected."},{"id":"regions","title":"Memory & Regions","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/regions.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"arena-model","text":"The arena model"},{"level":2,"id":"region-syntax","text":"Region syntax"},{"level":2,"id":"nested-regions","text":"Nested regions"},{"level":2,"id":"escaping-a-region","text":"Escaping a region: region.commit"},{"level":2,"id":"returning-from-regions","text":"Returning out of a region"},{"level":2,"id":"observability","text":"Observability"},{"level":2,"id":"region-caveats","text":"What you cannot rely on"}],"text":"Language Reference Memory & Regions The core of Vyne's memory model. Regions mark lexical scopes whose scratch allocations are released on exit. The arena model Every Vyne program runs on top of an arena — a bump allocator that hands out memory linearly and never calls free during the program's normal life. When you allocate something (an array, a string, a map, a struct), it goes at the current tip of the arena and the tip advances. That is the entire cost of allocation: a pointer bump and a bounds check. Memory is released in bulk, not individually. A region is a marker: \"everything allocated after this point is scratch, and I want it back when the block ends.\" On exit, the arena tip resets to where it was at region entry, and every allocation made inside is reclaimed in one operation. No garbage collector There is no GC. There is no reference counting during normal execution. There is only the arena and the region discipline you impose on it. Region syntax region.vy vyne copy through i :: 1 .. 10000 -> loop { region scratch { xs :: Array<Float64> = [ 1.0 , 2.0 , 3.0 ]; // ... heavy computation on xs ... }; // xs is gone. Arena tip is back where it was. }; Total memory usage stays flat across the loop, regardless of iteration count. Ten thousand iterations allocate the same arena bytes as one. Nested regions Regions nest. Each is a stack frame in the arena. Exiting the inner one releases only what the inner one allocated. nested.vy vyne copy region outer { a :: Array = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; // survives until outer closes region inner { b :: Array = [ 4 , 5 ]; // released at inner close }; // b is gone, a is still alive }; Escaping a region: region.commit If you need a value to survive past a region boundary, mark it with region.commit . This deep-clones the value into a separate arena that is not touched by region rewinds. commit.vy vyne copy lastMessage :: String = \"initial\" ; through epoch :: 1 .. 4 -> loop { region scratch { msg :: String = \"epoch:\" + string (epoch); lastMessage = msg; region.commit (lastMessage); }; out (lastMessage); // → epoch:1, epoch:2, epoch:3, epoch:4 }; Without the commit, the string allocated by \"epoch:\" + string(epoch) would be released when the region closed, and reading lastMessage afterwards would be a use-after-free. Returning out of a region The compiler handles return inside a region specially, depending on the return type: Return type Behaviour Int64, Float64, Bool, Null The value is copied by value; the region is rewound normally. Everything else The region's checkpoint is popped from the stack without rewinding, so the arena memory remains live for the caller to read. The enclosing region (if any) will eventually rewind. Observability vmem.total_allocated() reports the total number of bytes the arena has handed out. It is monotonic within a region and drops back to zero after a rewind. Use it to verify that your regions are actually keeping memory flat: verify.vy vyne copy module vmem; before :: Int64 = vmem.total_allocated(); through i :: 1 .. 1000 -> loop { region r { xs :: Array = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ]; }; }; after :: Int64 = vmem.total_allocated(); out (after - before); // → 0, if the region works What you cannot rely on Exact byte counts. The arena rounds allocations up to 8-byte boundaries and reserves entire blocks for its internal bookkeeping. total_allocated() is useful for verifying flatness, not for precise measurement. Aliasing across a rewind. Any pointer into arena memory that was allocated inside a region becomes invalid when the region closes. The compiler prevents this for variables it can trace, but it cannot catch every case — for example, a pointer smuggled into a struct field that outlives the region. Commit-arena growth. Every region.commit call clones its argument into the commit arena, which is only freed at program exit. Committing inside a hot loop grows the commit arena linearly with iterations. Commit in a hot loop If you write region.commit(x) inside a loop that runs a million times, the commit arena will hold a million clones by the time the loop ends. Only commit values that genuinely need to outlive the region, and prefer to commit outside the loop when possible."},{"id":"errors","title":"Error Handling","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/errors.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"try-catch","text":"try / catch"},{"level":2,"id":"finally","text":"finally"},{"level":2,"id":"throw","text":"throw"},{"level":2,"id":"defer","text":"defer"},{"level":2,"id":"interaction-with-regions","text":"Interaction with regions"},{"level":2,"id":"current-limitations","text":"Current limitations"}],"text":"Language Reference Error Handling try, catch, finally, throw, and defer. try / catch try must be followed by catch , finally , or both. The catch variable is bound in a scope visible only inside the catch block. try.vy vyne copy try { risky(); } catch (err) { out ( \"caught: \" + err); }; If you do not name the error variable, it defaults to _err : try-implicit.vy vyne copy try { risky(); } catch { out (_err); }; finally finally always runs — whether the try succeeded, was caught, or is propagating a new error. Use it for cleanup that must happen unconditionally. finally.vy vyne copy try { open_connection(); do_work(); } catch (err) { out ( \"error: \" + err); } finally { close_connection(); }; throw throw accepts an optional expression. Throwing with no argument passes null to the catch block. throw.vy vyne copy if (x < 0 ) { throw \"negative value not allowed\" ; }; // Bare throw — passes null throw ; defer defer stmt; queues a single statement to run when the enclosing function exits. Defers run in reverse order of declaration. defer.vy vyne copy fn process () { defer cleanup(); defer log( \"done\" ); // ... work happens here ... // on exit: log(\"done\"), then cleanup() }; defer placement defer is only supported at the top level of a function body. Nesting it inside an if , loop, or region is a compile error. Interaction with regions When a function returns from inside a region , the region is unwound before the caller sees the return value. If the return value is non-primitive, the region is popped without rewinding — see Memory & Regions for the full rule. When a function returns from inside a try block, the finally clause runs first. Current limitations break and continue cannot exit a try block. There is no way to re-throw the current exception with a fresh stack — throw err; works, but the stack context of the original throw is not preserved. Exceptions are values, not types. Any VyneValue can be thrown; there is no exception class hierarchy."},{"id":"builtins","title":"Built-ins","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/builtins.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"out","text":"out"},{"level":2,"id":"string-builtin","text":"string"},{"level":2,"id":"int64-float64-builtins","text":"int64 and float64"},{"level":2,"id":"type-builtin","text":"type"},{"level":2,"id":"sizeof-builtin","text":"sizeof"},{"level":2,"id":"sequence-builtin","text":"sequence"},{"level":2,"id":"map-builtin","text":"map"},{"level":2,"id":"exit-builtin","text":"exit"},{"level":2,"id":"free-builtin","text":"free"}],"text":"Language Reference Built-in Functions Always in scope. No module statement needed. out out(value); — writes the value to stdout followed by a newline. Accepts any VyneValue. Multiple arguments are allowed and each is printed on its own line. string string(v) -> String — converts any value to its display representation. Inverse operations are int64() and float64() . int64 and float64 int64(v) -> Int64 and float64(v) -> Float64 . Both accept numbers or strings. Parsing failures throw a runtime error. type type(v) -> String — returns the runtime type name of a value as a string: \"Int64\" , \"Array\" , \"Map\" , and so on. sizeof sizeof(v) -> Int64 — for arrays and strings, the element count or length. For maps, the number of entries. For primitives, always 8. sequence sequence(start, end) -> Array — returns a half-open array [start, start+1, ..., end-1] . Note that this differs from the .. range operator, which is inclusive. map map() -> Map — allocates a fresh empty map. Equivalent to writing {} . exit exit(code); — terminates the program immediately with the given exit code. Bypasses any active defers or finally blocks. free free(v); — a no-op in the current compiler. Because Vyne uses arena allocation and regions, individual values cannot be freed. The function exists for source compatibility with earlier drafts; the emitted C contains only a comment. Do not use it in new code; rely on regions instead. free() does nothing In the current backend, free(v) is compiled to a comment. It does not release memory. Do not depend on it, and do not expect C semantics."},{"id":"rulesets","title":"Rulesets","group":"Language Reference","path":"pages/rulesets.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"syntax","text":"Syntax"},{"level":2,"id":"available-flags","text":"Available flags"},{"level":2,"id":"strict-mode","text":"Strict mode"},{"level":2,"id":"warning-control","text":"Warning control"}],"text":"Language Reference Rulesets Compile-time and program-wide configuration. Three flags are currently implemented. Syntax A ruleset is written at the top of a file, before any executable code: ruleset.vy vyne copy ruleset { dynamic_casting }; ruleset { warnings }; // ... your program ... Or with a value: ruleset-value.vy vyne copy ruleset { memory_limit = 268435456 }; // 256 MB Available flags Flag Form Effect warnings flag or warnings = \"all\" \\| \"none\" \\| \"error_only\" Enables diagnostic output. Without this, warnings are suppressed and only errors surface. dynamic_casting flag Relaxes type checking. Type annotations on variables become optional and implicit conversions are permitted. Do not use in production. memory_limit flag or memory_limit = <bytes> Enables the memory-usage check hook. Currently reports but does not enforce. Reserved for a future runtime check. Strict mode By default, Vyne operates in strict mode : every variable must have an explicit :: Type annotation, and every assignment is type-checked against the declared type. The dynamic_casting flag is the only way to relax this, and it is intended for scripts and one-offs. Warning control When warnings is enabled, the compiler reports unused variables, shadowed variables, and a few other patterns. The granular ignore list form is: ignore.vy vyne copy ruleset { warnings = \"all\" , warnings_ignore = [ \"unused_variable\" , \"shadow_variable\" ] };"},{"id":"vcore","title":"VCore","group":"Standard Library","path":"pages/vcore.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"vcore-functions","text":"Functions"},{"level":2,"id":"vcore-properties","text":"Properties"}],"text":"Standard Library VCore System, time, platform, and process utilities. import vyne copy module vcore; Functions Runtime 5 functions vcore.now () → String Current local time as a formatted string \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\" . vcore.sleep (ms :: Int64 ) → Int64 Blocks the calling thread for ms milliseconds. Returns 1. vcore.platform () → String Compile-time platform string, e.g. \"Linux\" , \"Windows x64\" , \"Mac OSX\" . vcore.input ([prompt]) → String Reads one line from stdin, optionally printing a prompt first. Returns null at EOF. vcore.parse_array (s :: String ) → Array Parses a bracketed literal \"[1, 2, [3, 4]]\" into an Array. Supports nesting. vcore.hex_to_int64 (s :: String ) → Int64 Parses \"0xFF2D809A\" to Int64. Accepts optional 0x prefix. Properties Name Type Value vcore.version String Compiler version, e.g. \"v0.0.4-alpha\" vcore.engine String \"Vyne Native\" vcore.build String Build timestamp vcore.processor_count Int64 Number of logical CPUs vcore.pid Float64 Process ID (Float64 for interpreter parity) vcore.memory_usage Float64 Resident set size in bytes"},{"id":"vmath","title":"VMath","group":"Standard Library","path":"pages/vmath.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"vmath-constants","text":"Constants"},{"level":2,"id":"vmath-functions","text":"Functions"}],"text":"Standard Library VMath Numeric primitives: trigonometry, logarithms, ML activation functions, and a PCG32 random generator. import vyne copy module vmath; Constants Name Value vmath.pi 3.141592653589793 vmath.e 2.718281828459045 vmath.tau 6.283185307179586 vmath.phi 1.618033988749895 vmath.sqrt2 1.4142135623730951 vmath.inf IEEE infinity vmath.nan IEEE NaN Functions Numeric 30+ functions vmath.sqrt (x) → Float64 Square root. Also: abs , floor , ceil , round , exp , log , log10 . vmath.sin (x), cos (x), tan (x) → Float64 Trigonometric functions. Inverse forms: asin , acos , atan . Hyperbolic: sinh , cosh , tanh . vmath.pow (base, exp), hypot (a, b), atan2 (y, x) → Float64 Two-argument math functions. vmath.min (a, b), max (a, b), clamp (v, lo, hi) → Float64 Bounds helpers. clamp silently swaps arguments if lo > hi . vmath.sigmoid (x), relu (x) → Float64 Machine-learning activation functions. vmath.degrees (rad), radians (deg) → Float64 Angle unit conversion. vmath.random (lo, hi) → Int64 Uniform random integer in [lo, hi] inclusive. Uses PCG32 seeded from time() . vmath.random_float (lo, hi) → Float64 Uniform random float in [lo, hi) . Shares the RNG state with random . Random seeding The generator is seeded once on first call with a mix of time() and the address of the state variable. Two processes started in the same second will produce decorrelated output, but two runs in the same process always produce different sequences."},{"id":"vmem","title":"VMem","group":"Standard Library","path":"pages/vmem.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"vmem-functions","text":"Functions"}],"text":"Standard Library VMem Arena introspection. Use the total_allocated hook to verify your regions are flat. import vyne copy module vmem; Functions Inspection 4 functions vmem.total_allocated () → Int64 Total bytes handed out by the arena since program start, minus any reclaims. Drops back when a region rewinds. vmem.checkpoint () → Int64 Pushes an arena checkpoint and returns its handle. The region keyword uses this internally; you rarely need it directly. vmem.rewind (handle :: Int64 ) → Null Rewinds the arena to a previously captured checkpoint. vmem.reset () → Null Drops the entire arena. Only safe at top level when no live value refers to arena memory. Intended for tests. reset() is destructive vmem.reset() frees every arena block. Any VyneValue still pointing into the arena becomes a dangling reference. Do not call it in normal code."},{"id":"vglib","title":"VGLib","group":"Standard Library","path":"pages/vglib.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"vglib-constants","text":"Constants"}],"text":"Standard Library VGLib Hardware-accelerated 2D/3D graphics, built on Raylib. 8 modules, 65+ native bindings. import vyne copy module vglib; Core — window & frame loop 10 functions vglib.init ( width , height , fps , title , flags ) → Bool Initialises the window and target frame rate. vglib.running () → Bool True while the window is open. vglib.begin (), vglib.end () → Null Begin and end a drawing frame. Draw calls must be between them. vglib.clear ( color ) → Null Clears the background. Use vglib.rgba(r, g, b, a) to build the color integer. vglib.clear_gradient ( top , bottom ) → Null Fills the screen with a vertical gradient. vglib.close (), vglib.get_fps () → Null / Int64 Close the window; read the current FPS counter. 2D rendering — shapes, text, textures 12 functions vglib.line (x1, y1, x2, y2, color ), rect (x, y, w, h, color ), circle (x, y, r, color ) → Null Basic shape drawing primitives. vglib.text ( text , x, y, size , color ), text_ex ( font , text , x, y, size , color ) → Null Draw text with the default or a loaded font. vglib.load_font ( path ) → Font , measure_text ( font , text , size ) → [w, h] Font loading and measurement. vglib.load_texture ( path ) → Texture , draw_texture ( tex , x, y, w, h, color ) → Null Texture loading and drawing. Supports PNG, JPG, BMP, TGA. vglib.draw_phase_scope ( ... ), draw_spectrum_analyzer ( ... ), draw_scanlines ( spacing , color ) → Null Animated retro visualisation helpers. 3D rendering — cameras, meshes, models 20 functions vglib.camera ( fovy ) → Camera , begin3d ( cam ), end3d () → Null Create a camera and wrap 3D draw calls. vglib.line_3d (x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2, color ), cube (x, y, z, size , rot , color ), plane (x, y, z, w, h, color ), grid ( slices , spacing ) → Null Primitive 3D draw calls. vglib.load_model ( path ) → Model , draw_model ( model , x, y, z, scale , color ) → Null Load and draw OBJ / GLTF / IQM models. vglib.update_camera ( cam , mode ), get_pos ( cam ), set_pos ( cam , x, y, z), move_forward ( cam , dist ), move_right ( cam , dist ), rotate_view ( cam , sens ) → Null Camera controls for free-look and orbital modes. vglib.draw_instances ( model , instances ), draw_instances_ex ( model , instances , cam , dist ) → Null Instanced rendering with optional distance culling. Each instance is [x, y, z, scale] . Shaders & post-processing 8 functions vglib.load_shader ( path [, vs_path ]) → Shader Loads GLSL shader source. One path = fragment only; two = vertex + fragment. vglib.begin_shader ( shader ), end_shader () → Null Wrap draws in a shader. vglib.set_shader_value ( shader , uniform , value ) → Null Set a shader uniform. Accepts Float64 or [Float64, Float64] . vglib.load_render_texture (w, h), begin_texture_mode ( t ), end_texture_mode (), draw_canvas_scaled ( t ) → RenderTexture / Null Offscreen render targets for multi-pass effects. Map, AI, & collision 7 functions vglib.load_map ( path ) → Array Parses a .dat map file into [x, y, z, size] block descriptors. vglib.pathfind ( start , target , map , grid_size ) → [dx, dy, dz] AI obstacle avoidance. Returns a normalised direction vector. vglib.check_collision ( pos , size , cube_pos , cube_size ), check_collision_map ( pos , size , map ) → Bool Box-vs-box collision tests. vglib.export_obj ( map , filename ) → Bool Exports a map as Wavefront OBJ for external editing. vglib.get_ray_grid ( cam , dist , grid ), distance_3d (a, b) → [x, y, z] / Float64 Ray-grid snapping and Euclidean distance. Input — keyboard & mouse 10 functions vglib.key_down ( key ), key_pressed ( key ) → Bool key_down is true while held; key_pressed is true on the frame it was pressed. vglib.get_char (), get_key () → String / Int64 The last character or key code. vglib.mouse_down ( btn ), mouse_pos (), mouse_delta (), mouse_wheel () → Bool / [x, y] / Float64 Mouse state queries. vglib.disable_cursor (), enable_cursor () → Null Lock / unlock the cursor for FPS-style control. Constants Key codes ( vglib.KEY_A … vglib.KEY_DELETE ), color names ( vglib.COLOR_RED etc.), window flags ( vglib.FULLSCREEN , vglib.VSYNC ), camera modes ( vglib.CAMERA_FREE …), and mouse buttons ( vglib.MOUSE_LEFT …). All are read-only Int64 constants."},{"id":"vaudio","title":"VAudio","group":"Standard Library","path":"pages/vaudio.html","headings":[],"text":"Standard Library VAudio Professional-grade DSP toolkit: compressor, 7-band parametric EQ, FDN reverb, LUFS metering, BPM detection, and 3D spatial audio. 8 modules, 45+ bindings. import vyne copy module vaudio; Core — device & sounds 13 functions vaudio.init_audio () → Bool , close_audio () → Null Initialise / close the audio device. Defaults to 48 kHz, 32-bit float, stereo. vaudio.volume ( level ) → Bool Master volume in [0.0, 1.0] . vaudio.load_sound ( path ) → SoundHandle Load a WAV, MP3, FLAC, or OGG file into memory. vaudio.play_sound ( h ), pause_sound ( h ), resume_sound ( h ), stop_sound ( h ) → Bool Playback control. Supports overlapping instances. vaudio.is_playing ( h ), is_paused ( h ) → Bool Playback state queries. vaudio.sound_volume ( h , level ), set_pitch ( h , p ), seek_sound ( h , sec ) → Bool Per-sound volume, pitch (1.0 = normal), and seeking. Streaming — music & large files 10 functions vaudio.play_stream ( path ) → StreamHandle Streams a file from disk with looping. vaudio.update_stream ( h ) → Null Call every frame while streaming. vaudio.pause_stream ( h ), resume_stream ( h ), is_stream_playing ( h ), seek_stream ( h , sec ), get_stream_pos ( h ) → Bool / Float64 Stream playback control. get_stream_pos returns [0.0, 1.0] . vaudio.get_waveform ( path [, bins ]) → Array<Float64> Extracts normalised waveform peaks for visualisation. vaudio.attach_master_limiter ( h ) → Bool , set_dsp ( drive , mode ) → Null True-peak limiter (-0.9 dBFS) and global saturation. Compressor — dynamic range 5 functions vaudio.attach_compressor ( h ) → Bool Attach the compressor chain to a sound or stream. vaudio.set_compressor ( thresh , ratio , attack_ms , release_ms , makeup_db [, enabled [, auto_makeup ]]) → Bool Studio-grade parameter set. Threshold in dBFS, ratio as N:1 . vaudio.get_gr (), get_rms (), get_env () → Float64 Real-time metering: gain reduction in dB, normalised RMS, and envelope. Equalizer — 7-band parametric 5 functions vaudio.attach_eq ( h ) → Bool Attach the 7-band EQ. ISO frequencies: 60, 150, 400, 1000, 2500, 6000, 14000 Hz. vaudio.set_eq ( band , freq , gain_or_type , Q [, mode ]) → Bool Configure one band. Mode: 0=LPF, 1=HPF, 2=Peaking (default). vaudio.enable_eq ( on ) → Bool , get_spectrum () → Array<Float64> Global enable and 64-bin FFT spectrum for visualisation. vaudio.get_eq_peaks () → [lo, mid, hi] Weighted center frequencies of the low, mid, and high bands. Reverb — FDN algorithmic 3 functions vaudio.attach_reverb ( h ) → Bool , set_reverb ( decay , mix [, predelay_ms [, damping [, on ]]]) → Bool Four-line feedback delay network. Decay and mix in [0, 1] , predelay 0–100 ms, damping in [0, 1] . vaudio.get_analyzer_env () → Float64 Envelope follower output. Analysis — LUFS, BPM, offline render 5 functions vaudio.get_lufs () → Float64 ITU-R BS.1770 loudness measurement. vaudio.attach_bpm ( h ) → Bool , get_bpm ( h ) → Float64 Low-pass beat tracking in the 50–200 BPM range. vaudio.attach_analyzer ( h ) → Bool Real-time envelope and spectrum tracking. vaudio.render_offline ( input , output ) → Bool Processes an entire file through the DSP chain and writes a WAV. Applies EQ → compressor → saturation → reverb → limiter. 3D audio & saturation modes 1 function + 5 modes vaudio.sound_3d ( h , listener , source , max_dist [, max_vol ]) → Float64 Positions a sound in 3D space and returns the attenuation-weighted volume. Saturation modes for set_dsp ( drive , mode ) 0 Soft tube · 1 Hard clip · 2 Asymmetric · 3 Tape · 4 GameBoy (4-bit DAC + sample-rate reduction). DSP chain order When you attach multiple processors to the same sound or stream, they run in a fixed order: EQ → Compressor → Saturation → Reverb → Limiter . This matches typical mastering signal flow and cannot be changed at runtime."},{"id":"vserv","title":"VServ","group":"Standard Library","path":"pages/vserv.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"vserv-constants","text":"Status codes & MIME types"},{"level":2,"id":"vserv-example","text":"Complete example"}],"text":"Standard Library VServ HTTP / WebSocket server with Express-style routing. Native C under the hood, no external dependencies. import vyne copy module vserv; Core — create, route, listen 8 functions vserv.create_server ( port ) → ServerHandle Create an HTTP server bound to port . vserv.server_get ( srv , path , handler ), server_post , server_put , server_delete → Bool Register a route handler for a given method and path. vserv.server_use ( srv , middleware ) → Bool Register middleware that runs before every route. vserv.server_listen ( srv ) → Bool Starts the server. Blocks until stopped. vserv.serve_file ( path ) → Response Creates a response that serves a static file with the correct MIME type. Request & response 6 functions vserv.parse_request ( raw ) → Request Parses raw HTTP bytes into a structured Request. vserv.create_response () → Response Creates an empty 200 response. vserv.set_status ( res , code ), set_header ( res , key , value ) → Response Set status code and headers. Both return the response for chaining. vserv.send ( res [, body ]), send_json ( res , data ) → String Serialise the full HTTP response. send_json sets Content-Type: application/json . Express-style app & WebSocket 6 functions vserv.create_app () → AppHandle Create an Express-style app with built-in routing and middleware. vserv.app_get ( app , path , handler ), app_post ( app , path , handler ) → AppHandle Register routes. Returns the app for chaining. vserv.app_listen ( app , port ) → Bool Start the app on the given port. vserv.ws_upgrade ( req , res ) → Response Upgrade an HTTP request to a WebSocket connection. vserv.ws_send ( socket , message ) → Bool Send a message over a WebSocket. Framing handled automatically. Status codes & MIME types Read-only Int64 constants: vserv.OK , CREATED , ACCEPTED , NO_CONTENT , BAD_REQUEST , UNAUTHORIZED , FORBIDDEN , NOT_FOUND , METHOD_NOT_ALLOWED , SERVER_ERROR , and so on. MIME type strings: vserv.MIME_HTML , MIME_JSON , MIME_CSS , MIME_JS , MIME_PNG , MIME_JPG , MIME_WASM , MIME_MP4 , MIME_MP3 , and so on. Complete example api.vy vyne copy module vserv; module vcore; app :: vserv.AppHandle = vserv.create_app(); // Logger middleware fn logger (req, res, next) { vcore.out( \"[\" + req.method + \"] \" + req.path); next(); }; vserv.app_use(app, logger); // GET /hello vserv.app_get(app, \"/hello\" , fn (req, res) { vserv.send(res, \"Hello, World!\" ); }); // POST /api/users vserv.app_post(app, \"/api/users\" , fn (req, res) { vserv.send_json(res, { \"created\" : true , \"data\" : req.body }); }); vserv.app_listen(app, 3000 ); vcore.out( \"Server running on port 3000\" );"},{"id":"keywords","title":"Keywords","group":"Reference","path":"pages/keywords.html","headings":[],"text":"Reference Keywords Reserved words. None of these may be used as identifiers. Declarations & structure fn interface group module enum const region use deploy dismiss lib as ruleset Control flow if else while through loop collect filter every unique break continue return in Error handling try catch finally throw defer Ruleset flags warnings dynamic_casting memory_limit Literals true false null Built-in function names out string int64 float64 type sizeof sequence map exit free The three reserved word forms self , _err , and _ are treated specially by the parser but are not keywords."},{"id":"operators","title":"Operators","group":"Reference","path":"pages/operators.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"op-precedence","text":"Precedence table"},{"level":2,"id":"op-arithmetic","text":"Arithmetic"},{"level":2,"id":"op-logical","text":"Logical"},{"level":2,"id":"op-assignment-forms","text":"Special assignment forms"},{"level":2,"id":"op-reference","text":"Reference operators"}],"text":"Reference Operators Complete list, grouped by purpose, with precedence. Precedence table Level 1 binds loosest; level 13 binds tightest. Left/right indicates associativity. Level Operators Assoc. Description 1 ?: right Ternary conditional 2 ?? right Null coalesce 3 |> left Pipeline 4 || left Logical or (short-circuit) 5 && left Logical and (short-circuit) 6 == != left Equality (deep for composites) 7 in left Membership 8 < <= > >= left Comparison 9 .. left Range 10 + - // % left Additive, floor divide, modulo 11 * / ** left Multiplicative, power 12 ! - $ prefix Not, negation, address-of 13 ++ -- postfix Increment / decrement Arithmetic All arithmetic follows IEEE-754 for Float64 and two's-complement wraparound for Int64. Division and modulo by zero are runtime errors that terminate the program with a message. ** always produces a Float64 result, even for two Int64 operands. This matches the underlying pow() semantics and avoids the awkwardness of integer exponentiation overflow. Logical Short-circuit. The right-hand side is not evaluated if the result is already determined by the left. Any truthy value short-circuits || ; any falsy value short-circuits && . Special assignment forms Operator Meaning ??= Assign if current value is null ++ / -- Postfix increment / decrement on numeric lvalues Reference operators & and $ are related to pass-by-reference parameters. See the Functions chapter for usage; the parser recognises them but the C backend currently rejects reference variables at compile time."},{"id":"errcodes","title":"Error Codes","group":"Reference","path":"pages/errcodes.html","headings":[{"level":2,"id":"syntax-errors","text":"Syntax errors (VNE-001 – VNE-014)"},{"level":2,"id":"ruleset-errors","text":"Ruleset diagnostics (VNE-015 – VNE-032)"},{"level":2,"id":"advanced-errors","text":"Parser extensions (VNE-040 – VNE-050)"},{"level":2,"id":"reading-diagnostics","text":"Reading a diagnostic"}],"text":"Reference Error Codes The compiler emits structured diagnostics with stable codes. Use the code to look up the cause. Syntax errors (VNE-001 – VNE-014) Code Meaning VNE-001 Unexpected token VNE-002 Missing semicolon VNE-003 Unknown type name VNE-010 Warnings enabled VNE-011 Dynamic casting enabled VNE-012 Memory limit enabled VNE-013 Unknown ruleset flag VNE-014 Ruleset value expected Ruleset diagnostics (VNE-015 – VNE-032) Emitted when a ruleset is applied. These are informational notes, not errors. Parser extensions (VNE-040 – VNE-050) Code Meaning VNE-040 Malformed type parameter list on interface VNE-041 Nested generic in type parameter position VNE-042 Malformed type parameter list on function VNE-043 Nested generic in function type parameter VNE-050 Malformed region.commit — expected \"commit\" after \"region.\" Reading a diagnostic Every diagnostic in the terminal is printed with: The severity (error, warning, note, perf hint). The error code, which you can look up here. The message text. The source location, in file:line form. A three-line excerpt from the offending file with a caret pointing at the column. Zero or more suggestions, when the compiler has a specific fix in mind. If you see an error that is not in this list, please open an issue — an undocumented error code is a bug in the documentation, not in your code."}];
